today there are a dozen companies with a  market cap above 500 billion dollars  most of which are tech companies but  none of these companies were actually  the first company to reach the 500  billion mark the first company to reach  this milestone was actually cisco way  back in 2000 at the peak of the dot-com  bubble this made cisco the most valuable  company in the world but after the  dot-com bubble burst cisco lost 77  percent of its value and it has never  recovered to its former peaks looking at  the stock graph it looks like cisco has  been making positive progress but this  is actually rather deceiving you see  cisco has been aggressively buying back  their stock over the past 20 years so  the stock has to reach much higher  levels for the company to be worth the  same amount currently the price to beat  is 118 dollars meaning that cisco has to  rally nearly 100 percent just to beat  its dot-com peak so here's how cisco  became the world's most valued company  and her fall from grace  taking a look back the story of cisco  dates back to a stanford couple named  sandy lerner and leonard bosack sandy  was the director of computer facilities  and leonard was in charge of managing  the computers throughout the early 1980s  stanford students and staff commonly  used something called the blue box to  link all the computers in the school the  blue box was designed in-house by andy  bechtosheim and william yeager and he  developed the circuitry while william  developed the software the blue box was  just a simple multi-protocol router but  40 years ago this was some pretty  advanced tech with significant  commercial potential however william did  not want to sell the blue box  commercially and andy had already  co-founded sun microsystems so sandy and  leonard decided to commercialize a  product themselves and with that the duo  launched cisco on december 10 1984. the  name cisco was a reference to san  francisco and the logo is of course the  golden gate bridge despite the quick  start the early days were not smooth by  any means you see it neither stanford  nor william were happy to hear that  these two had stolen their technology  and were trying to profit from it  william claims that he never gave  permission to sell the technology  commercially and stanford contemplated  five criminal charges against cisco for  intellectual property theft for years  but eventually the two parties would  meet in the middle and stanford would  agree to license the technology to cisco  in 1987. with stanford on their side the  couple could freely sell the router  which was extremely lucrative by the end  of 1987 cisco was selling 250 000 worth  of routers every month but the couple  had a much larger vision for the company  so they partnered with venture  capitalist don valentine and sekia  capital during this funding round though  the couple lost majority control of the  company and this was the beginning of  the end for the duo  well quite simply i got fired sooner or  later the venture capitalists always  want to get rid of the founders  that's just part of don's formula don  valentine brought in a new ceo named  john morgridge and this guy did not get  along with the founders at all  first of all he went ahead and replaced  several of the managers at cisco many of  whom were friends with the founders and  after cisco went public in february of  1990 he went ahead and fired sandy  herself leonard would instantly resign  from cisco in protest but this didn't  really accomplish anything the couple  ended up selling all their stock for 100  million dollars and to put that in  perspective their 30 stake would have  been worth 150 billion dollars at the  peak of the bubble  so yeah sandy and leonard got screwed  over but in a way it's actually quite  poetic the couple stole the technology  from stanford and the vc stole the  company from them  though the founders were completely out  this wasn't a bad thing by any means  while john morgridge had a lot of beef  with the founders he knew exactly how to  grow the company at first john targeted  corporate clients with large scientific  departments cisco offered to unify their  science departments by linking all of  their tech together  around the same time local area networks  or lands were blowing up in popularity  and cisco became one of the go-to  companies for setting up and the linking  lands between 1991 and 1992 cisco sales  nearly doubled from 183.2 million  dollars to 339.6 million dollars  similarly their net profit jumped from  43.2 million dollars to 84.4 million  dollars this explosive growth won cisco  the title of second fastest growing  company in the us but it didn't take  long for mainstream competitors to enter  the scene with the biggest one being ibm  you see ibm had been growing their own  network business for years called system  network architecture or sna  sna was a proprietary network that was  specific to ibm computers and given that  sna was limited to ibm computers cisco  more or less ignored the network but in  september of 1992 ibm announced that  they were going to license their  peer-to-peer networking protocol used in  sna  cisco instantly fired back claiming that  they were going to modify their own  peer-to-peer networking protocol so that  it can be used with sna  despite this fiery start though cisco  gave in within just 12 months and ended  up helping ibm develop and license the  protocol  aside from cooperating with ibm cisco  decided to partner with atnt as well  in 1993 we saw the emergence of a new  data communications protocol called atm  or asynchronous transfer mode  atm allowed for high speed transfers of  data voice video and images without the  use of routers instead of finding the  new technology and embracing routers  cisco decided to enter a joint  development project with atnta and  stratacom together they developed the  standards for the new technology and  ensured that it could be used with  existing networks these high-level  partnerships combined with the rapidly  growing network space allowed cisco to  continue flying high throughout the  1990s by 1998 cisco crossed the 100  billion mark in market cap and in 1999  they would go on an acquisition spree  they bought a total of 17 companies that  year with valuations as high as 6.9  billion dollars  it seemed like a trillion evaluation was  just around the corner but then the  dot-com bubble burst and that marked the  end of cisco's domination  though cisco's stock price plummeted the  executives were still quite optimistic  about the future after all it's not like  the crash was specific to cisco so cisco  turned their focus onto new avenues for  growth which led them to the consumer  space back in the early 1990s regular  consumers had basically no need for  routers and network devices but with  rapid internet adoption everyday  consumers were quickly becoming a larger  part of the picture so in 2003 cisco  spent 500 million dollars to acquire the  consumer router and the network company  linksys soon after they launched their  human networking advertising campaign  this campaign basically marketed cisco's  vision of not just linking together  technology but linking together humans  from around the world  it's not really clear how effective the  campaign was at increasing sales but  glancing at the comments it looks like  people really like the music they used  in the ads  anyway aside from targeting everyday  consumers cisco also looked for new ways  they could target corporate customers  they had already linked together  infrastructure so the next natural step  was to link together people and that  brings us to the webex acquisition in  march of 2007 cisco acquired webex for  3.2 billion dollars and this got cisco  into the web conferencing space on top  of all this cisco was also trying to  expand into emerging markets for example  cisco spent one billion dollars to  establish globalization center east in  bangalore india they also purchased  several companies from emerging markets  such as darned networks despite all  their efforts though cisco hasn't really  gone anywhere in a long time  if you look at their revenue graph it  hasn't moved in a decade and their net  income graph doesn't paint a better  picture either so what happened  one of the biggest factors holding back  cisco is being spread too then  throughout the early 2000s cisco  constantly focused on expanding into new  sectors and industries but they didn't  execute any of these particularly well  for example remember how cisco bought  lynxes for 500 million in 2003 well they  sold it to belkin in 2013. the  acquisition amount was not revealed but  it likely wasn't a favorable for cisco  similarly the webex acquisition didn't  go too well either cisco's vice  president of engineering eric yuan  suggested transforming webex into a  mobile friendly video system and given  that eric was one of webex's first 20  employees and knew the industry inside  and out it probably would have been a  good idea to listen to him but instead  cisco rejected his idea and eric ended  up quitting and founding zoom as you can  see it's not like cisco was entering bad  industries in fact they were entering  great industries but they weren't  executing well in any industry which  made it easier for competitors to  overtake them speaking of competitors we  have cisco's second shortfall which is  market saturation one of the keys to  cisco's fast growth was that there was  very little competition in the 80s and  90s but now the server router and  networking markets are extremely  saturated basically every tech company  you can think of has their own server  business whether that be netgear dell  huawei or lenovo this increased  competition has made it much more  difficult to secure market share and  shine above the rest but by far the  biggest problem with cisco is that their  core business is losing relevance  historically the core of cisco has been  selling networking hardware to corporate  customers but many corporate customers  no longer need cisco's hardware instead  of buying hardware from cisco and using  the hardware to offer cloud and service  solutions corporate customers are just  building their own hardware companies  like google amazon and microsoft have no  desire to rely on cisco for hardware now  it's not like they've caught all  relationships with cisco but with time  they're becoming more and more  independent and unless cisco finds a new  way to appeal to these customers it  won't take long for this stagnation to  turn into a decline  despite all these shortfalls and lack of  growth cisco is still one of the biggest  companies in the world currently they  hover around the low 30s on the fortune  500 list with a market cap of 250  billion dollars looking forward while  cisco's networking future isn't so clear  many cisco investors argue that cisco is  no longer a networking company even  though a majority of cisco's revenue  still comes from providing  infrastructure cisco investors argue  that what's more important is where  cisco is investing their money and the  vast majority of cisco's investments  today are in services applications and  security whether these investments will  actually pan out is yet to be seen but  even if they don't cisco is by no means  in danger of collapsing they're still  extremely profitable and it would take  decades for such a company to fall but  if your shift away from networking  doesn't work out it becomes more and  more unlikely that cisco will ever  return to their former glory but that's  just what i think do you guys think  cisco can make a comeback to the top 5  or top 10 companies in the world comment  that down below  also drop a like if you're glad that you  didn't buy cisco stock in 2000  and of course consider joining our  discord community to suggest future  video ideas and consider subscribing to  more questions logically answered but  until then i'm ari and i'll see you guys  on the next one  you

